  <actuator>
    <!-- ADDITION 6 (2026-07-28): arm gains kp x2, kd x0.7 vs menagerie's stock
         (4500/-450, 3500/-350, 2000/-200).

         WHY. Menagerie tunes these for a free-standing arm, and the result is heavily
         OVERDAMPED: with an effective joint inertia around 2.5 kg m^2, kp=4500 kd=450
         gives zeta ~ 2.1, so the dominant time constant is tau ~ 0.15 s against a 0.05 s
         control tick. The arm therefore realises only ~33% of each commanded delta and
         sits at a standing gravity sag. Because the collector labels every tick with
         (target - current)/DELTA_POS_SCALE, that sag is written into EVERY training
         label as a constant offset -- measured +0.174 on dx and +0.127 on dz in the a1
         dataset, against a released LIBERO distribution that is symmetric about zero.
         Worse, it made dx one-sided (q01 = -0.08 over 8700 frames), so the data carried
         no signal for retreating in -x.

         This is not fixable by slowing the reference trajectory -- measured, that makes
         the ratio WORSE (0.32 -> 0.15 at 0.25x speed), because the sag is static and
         does not scale with reference velocity while the commanded motion does.

         WHY THIS IS A MOVE TOWARD LIBERO, NOT AWAY. LIBERO drives the Panda with
         robosuite's OSC, a force-based controller that tracks its Cartesian setpoint
         tightly. Our stiff-IK Route A stand-in tracking only a third of its setpoint is
         the artefact; making it actually arrive is closer to the plant the checkpoint
         was trained against, not further from it.

         MEASURED, 8 episodes/config, reach + noise (probe_gains.py):
           config       placed  realised/commanded  mean_dx  q01_dx  clip%  table_Fn
           baseline      5/8          0.355          0.142   -0.075  0.34     0.0 N
           kp2 kd0.7     4/8          0.724          0.084   -0.181  0.17     0.0 N   <- this
           kp3 kd0.6     4/8          0.899          0.077   -0.243  0.65     0.0 N
           kp4 kd0.5     4/8          0.980          0.097   -0.258  1.05     0.0 N
           kp6 kd0.45    4/8          1.040          0.121   -0.333  1.60     0.0 N
         Stiffer than kp2 starts to ring: mean_dx turns back UP past kp3 and dz begins
         saturating at 1.000. kp2 kd0.7 is the knee -- it halves the bias, more than
         doubles tracking, has the LOWEST clipping of any config tested, and is the first
         config where dx is meaningfully two-sided. Task success is unchanged within the
         sample. Contact into the table stays at 0 N because --min-clearance still holds
         the commanded target above the surface.

         IT ALSO HALVES THE STATIC SAG AT RESET, which is a second, independent win.
         eef height above the table at LIBERO's reset joints, table top = 0.9000:
           pure forward kinematics, no dynamics   0.2728
           stock  kp4500 kd450                    0.2680   sag 4.84 mm
           this   kp9000 kd315                    0.2704   sag 2.44 mm
           (x4    kp18000 kd225                   0.2716   sag 1.23 mm)
         LIBERO's measured value is 0.2733. Note that libero/README.md and
         fine_tune/README.md both quote "ours 0.2728" against that, a claimed 0.5 mm
         match -- but 0.2728 is the PURE FK number. The pose the arm actually settles at
         and starts every episode from was 0.2680, a 5.3 mm error. This change takes it
         to 2.9 mm.

         NOTE: this changes the plant, so it changes libero_closed_loop.py at inference
         too. That is REQUIRED, not incidental -- the collector's whole design rule is
         that labels are produced through the same controller that will consume them.
         It also means a1/ and a2/ were collected on the old plant and are superseded by
         a3/. panda.xml is untouched, so the DROID path is unaffected. -->
    <general class="panda" name="actuator1" joint="joint1" gainprm="4500" biasprm="0 -4500 -450"/>
    <general class="panda" name="actuator2" joint="joint2" gainprm="4500" biasprm="0 -4500 -450"
      ctrlrange="-1.7628 1.7628"/>
    <general class="panda" name="actuator3" joint="joint3" gainprm="3500" biasprm="0 -3500 -350"/>
    <general class="panda" name="actuator4" joint="joint4" gainprm="3500" biasprm="0 -3500 -350"
      ctrlrange="-3.0718 -0.0698"/>
    <general class="panda" name="actuator5" joint="joint5" gainprm="2000" biasprm="0 -2000 -200" forcerange="-12 12"/>
    <general class="panda" name="actuator6" joint="joint6" gainprm="2000" biasprm="0 -2000 -200" forcerange="-12 12"
      ctrlrange="-0.0175 3.7525"/>
    <general class="panda" name="actuator7" joint="joint7" gainprm="2000" biasprm="0 -2000 -200" forcerange="-12 12"/>
    <!-- Remap original ctrlrange (0, 0.04) to (0, 255): 0.04 * 100 / 255 = 0.01568627451 -->
    <!-- ADDITION 5 (2026-07-28): forcerange -100..100 -> -20..20, robosuite's own value
         (panda_gripper.xml: `forcelimited="true" forcerange="-20 20"` on each finger).
         Menagerie's 100 N is 5x that. Changed on the same principle as the rest of this
         file -- use robosuite's number, not menagerie's -- but be aware it is NOT load
         bearing: swapping it produced bit-identical trajectories, because at a seated
         grasp the tendon is only pulling a few newtons and never reaches either limit.
         The ball-extrusion problem it was reached for turned out to be contact softness,
         and is fixed on the ball geom in the scene file instead. -->
    <general class="panda" name="actuator8" tendon="split" forcerange="-20 20" ctrlrange="0 255"
      gainprm="0.01568627451 0 0" biasprm="0 -100 -10"/>
  </actuator>

  <keyframe>
    <key name="home" qpos="0 0 0 -1.57079 0 1.57079 -0.7853 0.04 0.04" ctrl="0 0 0 -1.57079 0 1.57079 -0.7853 255"/>
  </keyframe>

  <contact>
    <exclude body1="link0" body2="link1"/>
  </contact>
</mujoco>
